"""Plain-language reference used by the HTML user guide and control tooltips."""
HELP = {
    "controls": ("Controls to simulate", "Load the rig controls you want to add overlap to. For a chain, put the top control first and the bottom control last. Animate the driver or parent first: a chain at rest needs source movement to create a swing."),
    "load": ("Load selected", "Select controls in Maya, then click this to put them in the list. Selected parents are placed before their children. Loading a new selection clears this session's preview and endpoint; baked keys remain."),
    "up": ("Up", "Move the highlighted list entry one place earlier. Use this to arrange the chain from its root toward its tip. This changes the simulation order, not the rig hierarchy."),
    "down": ("Down", "Move the highlighted list entry one place later. Parent controls must appear before their selected children. The last control uses the endpoint to define its final segment."),
    "mode": ("Mode", "Rotation adds swing by turning the controls, useful for tails, ears, and pendulums. Translation adds lag to their positions, useful for floating props or controls that should follow a moving target."),
    "channels": ("Apply overlap to", "Choose which local control channels receive overlap. For a pendulum that bends around its local X axis, select Rotate X only. Unchecked axes keep their source animation and receive no new keys. The choice applies to every loaded control."),
    "range": ("Start / end", "The first and last frames to preview and bake. Include time after the driver stops so the swing can settle. Bake replaces the keys inside this range; keys outside it are kept. Extend Maya's playback range if needed."),
    "frequency": ("Stiffness", "How quickly the spring follows the original motion. Lower values give slower movement and more lag, which can feel heavier. Higher values follow faster and feel tighter. Hz describes the spring's response rate; it is not a mass setting. Try 1.5 Hz for a restrained pendulum."),
    "damping": ("Damping ratio", "How quickly the swing loses energy. Lower values keep it bouncing longer; raising it toward 1 suppresses bouncing. Values above 1 can return to rest more slowly, with little oscillation. Try 0.35 for a longer swing with some bounce, or 0.5 for quicker settling. Very low damping can produce large swings."),
    "strength": ("Effect strength", "Blend between the original motion and the simulated overlap. 0 keeps the original motion; 1 uses the full effect. Lower this if the swing is too large. A value such as 0.65 gives a more restrained effect without changing the spring settings."),
    "substeps": ("Minimum substeps", "The minimum number of small simulation steps taken inside each frame. More steps improve accuracy during fast movement but take longer to calculate. This controls simulation quality, not weight or bake key spacing. 16 is a useful starting point; the solver may use more automatically."),
    "preroll": ("Preroll frames", "Evaluate this many frames before Start to prepare the spring's initial state. Those extra frames are not baked. It uses the existing animation before Start; it does not invent extra movement. 12 is a useful starting point."),
    "length": ("Fallback tip length", "The distance from the final control's rotation pivot to its virtual endpoint, measured in scene units. It is used in Manual mode, or when Auto has no preceding selected segment. It helps define the lever for rotation. Auto chain placement or a custom endpoint overrides it."),
    "tip_mode": ("Endpoint direction", "Auto continues the previous chain segment beyond the final pivot, so a hanging chain gets an endpoint below its last control. Manual uses the fallback axis and length instead. A custom locator takes priority. After changing this, replace an existing endpoint locator and refresh source."),
    "axis": ("Fallback tip axis", "The local direction used for a manual endpoint, such as -Y for a downward segment. This is the segment's direction, not the channel you want to rotate. A Rotate X hinge needs its endpoint off the X axis. Auto chain placement or a custom locator overrides this setting."),
    "tip": ("Create / replace endpoint", "Create a movable locator for the final segment, or replace this session's existing locator using the current direction settings. Place it toward the end of the hanging part, beyond the last pivot. Do not reload the selection with this locator selected. Refresh source after moving it."),
    "save": ("Save preset", "Save the spring settings, output axes, endpoint mode, and bake optimization options to a JSON file. The preset does not store rig control names, frame range, or a custom locator's position."),
    "load_preset": ("Load preset", "Restore settings from a saved JSON preset. Your rig controls are kept. Check the endpoint and refresh source afterward. Older presets keep their manual axis/length behavior and default to all output axes and an unoptimized bake."),
    "refresh": ("Refresh source + preview", "Read the current source animation, solve the spring, and build animated locator previews. Use this after editing source keys, parents, frame range, or endpoint placement. To redo a pass, Undo the bake first so source is sampled without it. The original rig is driven only after Bake."),
    "preview": ("Update preview from cached source", "Reuse the source animation already sampled, then calculate a new preview with your current stiffness, damping, strength, substeps, or output axes. It is faster than sampling again. If you changed source animation or endpoint placement, use Refresh source instead."),
    "optimize": ("Optimize bake", "On by default. Keys only where the motion needs them, keeping the first/last keys and sampled swing peaks. The simulation stays at full quality. With this off, every frame is keyed. With it on, the tolerance controls how closely the reduced curves follow the full bake."),
    "tolerance": ("Bake tolerance", "The maximum allowed difference per baked channel when optimization is on. Rotation uses degrees; translation uses scene distance units. Lower values retain more keys; higher values permit fewer. Start at 0.25 degrees. This is a channel error limit, not the total world-space error at the end of a chain."),
    "bake": ("Bake to controls", "Key the previewed motion directly onto the controls' own animation curves, replacing their keys inside the frame range. No animation layer, blend node, or other Overlap node stays connected, and the preview locators and endpoint are removed. Only checked channels are keyed. Channels driven by animation layers are refused: delete old Overlap layers or merge layers first. Optimization settings can change after preview. One Undo reverts the whole bake; refresh source before another pass."),
    "clear": ("Clear session helpers", "Remove this window's preview locators and endpoint, and discard its cached source and simulation. The loaded control list and settings stay. Baked keys and the rig remain in the scene."),
    "delete_bake": ("Delete bake", "Overlap 0.4 and earlier baked onto animation layers. Choose one of those layers from the Legacy layer list, then click Delete bake to remove it and its curves, preserving original source keys and the rig. Maya Undo restores a deleted layer. Refresh source before baking a replacement."),
    "shelf": ("Add to shelf", "Choose your preferred Maya shelf, then use File > Add to current shelf. Overlap adds its custom 32 by 32 icon and saves the shelf. Running this again updates the existing Overlap button on that shelf instead of adding another. Keep the tool folder in its installed location."),
}
